## 2. Methods

### 2.1 Cohort

Synthetic patients are generated from population priors packaged with the platform
(`resources/genomic_priors.yaml`): ancestry mixture, comorbidity hazards modulated by age,
BMI and smoking, baseline physiology, concomitant medication and illustrative
pharmacogenomic markers (CYP2D6/CYP3A4 phenotype, HLA-B\*57:01, HLA-DQ2.2, ADRB1 Arg389Gly,
ACE I/D, SLCO1B1). No real patient data is used.

### 2.2 Pharmacokinetics and pharmacodynamics

A one-compartment oral model with first-order absorption and superposition across repeated
dosing ({{ protocol.epoch_duration_hours }} h per epoch):

```
C(t) = F*D*ka / (V*(ka-ke)) * (exp(-ke*t) - exp(-ka*t)),  ke = ln(2) / t_half
```

Individual clearance integrates body weight (allometric), an age-related decline after 40
years, an eGFR-scaled renal share and a CYP2D6-scaled hepatic share
(renal {{ protocol.drug.renal_fraction | num }}, hepatic {{ protocol.drug.hepatic_fraction | num }},
t½ {{ protocol.drug.half_life_h | num }} h, EC50 {{ protocol.drug.ec50_mg_l | num }} mg/L).
Biomarker effects follow a sigmoid Emax link on the average steady-state concentration; QTc
and hepatotoxicity use peak concentration.

### 2.3 Learned model component

A ridge-regression residual head (trained on synthetic historical cohorts, see
`insilico-trial-mas train-physiology`) corrects the mechanistic prediction for
pharmacogenomic interactions and saturating exposure effects. Model version
`{{ provenance.physiology_model_version }}`, digest `{{ provenance.physiology_model_digest or "n/a" }}`,
backend `{{ provenance.physiology_backend }}`.

### 2.4 Agent roles

| Agent | Responsibility in this run |
| --- | --- |
| Protocol Agent | Eligibility screening, stratified block randomisation, per-epoch dosing directives, protocol deviations |
| Patient Persona Agent | Individual PK/PD, biomarker prediction, adverse-event sampling, LLM narration of symptoms, adherence |
| Biostatistician Agent | Aggregation, inference, safety monitoring, this report |

### 2.5 Statistics

Continuous endpoints: Welch t-test with a seeded bootstrap confidence interval for the
difference of means. Binary endpoints: two-proportion z-test (Fisher exact when any cell is
< 5) with a Newcombe hybrid-score interval. Proportions carry Wilson intervals. Secondary
endpoints are controlled for multiplicity with the Benjamini-Hochberg procedure
(q-values reported). All statistics are computed without a SciPy dependency, so results are
bit-reproducible across environments.

